      "latency_ms": 2658.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 630,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the foundation of all known life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through a few different lenses:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Scientific & Physical Properties\nWater is unique because it defies many standard rules of chemistry and physics:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **\"The Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure (which creates a slight positive charge on the hydrogen side and a negative charge on the oxygen side), water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances. This is why things like salt and sugar dissolve easily in it.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats, which is crucial for aquatic life (lakes freeze from the top down, allowing fish to survive underneath).\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps living bodies from overheating.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, primarily in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n* Only about **2.5% of Earth's water is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. \n* Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and streams.\n* Water is constantly moving in a continuous cycle called the **hydrological cycle** (evaporation $\\rightarrow$ condensation $\\rightarrow$ precipitation $\\rightarrow$ collection). The water you drink today is the exact same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nBiology cannot exist without water. \n* **Within Organisms:** The human body is made up of about 60% water. Water acts as a cushion for organs, regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, and acts as the transport system for nutrients and waste via blood.\n* **Evolution:** Life began in the oceans roughly 3.8 billion years ago, and every living thing—from plants to animals—depends on water to drive cellular processes. \n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It shapes the geography of our planet, drives our weather systems, and is the literal lifeblood of every living creature."
